@media (max-width: 576px) {.article--viewer .a-wrapper .content .text iframe {height: 900px;}}Calculating Simple InterestSimple interest on any sum of money is calculated using the steps discussed below,Simple interest is found by using the formula SI = (PRT)/100. Step 1: The principal(P), Rate of interest(R) and time(T) of loan amount is noted.Step 2: Use the formula SI = (P × R × T / 100) to calculate Simple InterestStep 3: Use all the values from Step 1 and substitute them in Step 2.Step 4: Simplify the value obtained in Step 3 to get required simple interest.Let's consider an example to understand the procedure better.Example: Find the SI on ₹10000 deposited for 3 years at 5% per annum.Adam deposits $8,000 in a savings account at a simple interest rate of 7% per annum for 3 years. What will be the simple interest earned, and what will be the total amount after 3 years?Solution: P = $8,000R = 7% per annumT = 3 yearsSI = (P × R × T/100)SI = 8000 × 7 × 3 / 100SI = 168000/100 = 1680Total Amount = Principal + InterestTotal Amount = 8000 + 1680 = $9680Shortcuts and Tricks for Simple InterestPractice Questions on Simple InterestSimple Interest QuizDifference between Simple and Compound Interest
